<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>

  <!-- The grenade throw, in three clips covering four facings.

       This whole folder only exists when Melee Animation does - see loadFolders.xml - because
       AM.AnimDef lives in their assembly and a def naming a class that is not loaded is a red
       error on startup rather than a skipped def.

       A pawn body is not drawn at a free angle. Their DrawPawns hands the pawn renderer a Rot4
       taken from the clip's PawnBody.Direction, so the body snaps to one of RimWorld's four
       facing sprites. The arm and the grenade are ordinary sprites drawn at a real angle and can
       sweep anywhere, but the body cannot - which is why one clip cannot serve every direction.

       East is mirrored horizontally for west. North and south each have their own clip,
       with the step, supporting hand and draw depth authored for that facing.

       Every throw clip names RimArt.MeleeAnimation.ThrowAimWorker as its rendererWorker. The clip
       only covers its own direction; the worker turns the throwing hand and the held item to the
       exact target angle when they are drawn (up to 45 degrees). It lives in
       Patch_MeleeAnimation/1.6/Assemblies, which loads only with Melee Animation, because it
       subclasses their AnimationRendererWorker.

       The json is written by make_throw_anim.py in the mod root, not by the Unity editor their
       tutorial describes. Their loader does not care which wrote it: AnimDataSourceManager
       scans every active mod for an Animations folder and indexes what it finds by filename. -->
  <AM.AnimDef>
    <defName>AG_ThrowGrenade</defName>
    <label>Throw: grenade</label>
    <description>Throwing a grenade.</description>
    <jobString>Throwing a grenade.</jobString>
    <type>Other</type>
    <pawnCount>1</pawnCount>
    <data>RimArt_ThrowGrenade.json</data>
    <rendererWorker>RimArt.MeleeAnimation.ThrowAimWorker</rendererWorker>

    <cellData>
      <li>
        <type>PawnStart</type>
        <position>(0, 0)</position>
        <pawnIndex>0</pawnIndex>
      </li>
    </cellData>

    <!-- Without this the pawn throws with invisible hands.

         Melee Animation decides hand visibility in ConfigureHandsForPawn as
         `animation ?? weapon ?? false`, and a thrower is not holding a melee weapon, so the
         weapon term is null and the whole expression falls through to false. Saying it here is
         the only way a weaponless animation ever draws hands. Their renderer still supplies the
         hand sprite, the pawn's own skin colour, and a glove if the pawn is wearing one. -->
    <handsVisibility>
      <li>
        <pawnIndex>0</pawnIndex>
        <showMainHand>true</showMainHand>
        <showAltHand>true</showAltHand>
      </li>
    </handsVisibility>

    <!-- Not an execution, so there is no probability for a player to weight it against other
         animations - it plays whenever the ability is cast or it does not play at all. -->
    <canEditProbability>false</canEditProbability>
  </AM.AnimDef>

